This guide explains how to use the Atlassian CLI (ACLI) to create and manage Jira issues programmatically with proper formatting, custom fields, and team assignments in the Coincover Jira instance. Unless explicitly stated otherwise you should use the CLI to directly interact with Jira.

# Language
When writing epic and story descriptions use tempered factual language, avoid grandiose language, superluous adjectives or promises that are not guaranteed to be met.

# Practical Advice
- Epics are short lived and should be used to group related stories that are related to a single feature or problem.
- Stories are operational and reflect the work required to complete the epic.
- Optimise for fewer stories within any given epic. Related work should be grouped together into a single story if possible. They can be decomposed into smaller stories later if needed.
- Use the /out directory to stage JSON payloads if required.

# Prerequisites
ACLI installed: brew install acli (macOS) or follow Atlassian CLI installation guide
Authentication: Run acli auth login to authenticate with Coincover's Jira instance
Project access: User must have permissions to create/edit issues in the target project
⚠️ CRITICAL: Custom Field IDs
BOTH fields are REQUIRED for ALL stories in the CCE project. Using the wrong ID in the wrong field will cause creation to fail.

🔴 IMPORTANT: These are TWO DIFFERENT FIELDS
There are TWO separate custom fields that you must set correctly:

Super-Team Field = customfield_10972 (which team owns this work)
Component Field = customfield_10970 (which product/area this affects)
❌ DO NOT MIX THESE UP! Using a Team ID in the Component field or vice versa will cause errors.

## Quick Reference Tables
📋 Super-Team Field (customfield_10972)
Field ID: customfield_10972
Purpose: Assigns which engineering team owns this work

Team Name	✅ CORRECT ID	Use For
Stallions	11559	Core platform team work
Control-Freaks	11560	Control platform team work (Recovery, ACL, Portal)
(Infra)	11561	Infrastructure & DevOps team work
Usage in JSON:

"additionalAttributes": {
  "customfield_10972": {
    "id": "11560"  ← TEAM ID GOES HERE (e.g., Control-Freaks)
  }
}
🏗️ Component Field (customfield_10970)
Field ID: customfield_10970
Purpose: Identifies which product/codebase component this affects

Component Name	✅ CORRECT ID	Use For
Control Interface	11554	User-facing Control Portal features
Aave	11555	Aave integration work
Cooper Integration	11556	Cooper platform integration
Canton/Cumberland	11557	Canton/Cumberland specific features
Infra	11558	Infrastructure/DevOps components
Usage in JSON:

"additionalAttributes": {
  "customfield_10970": {
    "id": "11554"  ← COMPONENT ID GOES HERE (e.g., Control Interface)
  }
}
⚠️ Common Mistake: Mixing Up Field IDs
❌ WRONG - This will FAIL:
"additionalAttributes": {
  "customfield_10972": {
    "id": "11554"  ← ERROR! This is a COMPONENT ID, not a TEAM ID!
  },
  "customfield_10970": {
    "id": "11560"  ← ERROR! This is a TEAM ID, not a COMPONENT ID!
  }
}
✅ CORRECT - This will work:
"additionalAttributes": {
  "customfield_10972": {
    "id": "11560"  ← TEAM: Control-Freaks (correct field)
  },
  "customfield_10970": {
    "id": "11554"  ← COMPONENT: Control Interface (correct field)
  }
}
📝 Decision Tree: Which IDs Should I Use?
Step 1: Choose Your Team (customfield_10972)
Is this work for...
├─ Control platform (Recovery, ACL, Portal)? → Use 11560 (Control-Freaks)
├─ Core wallet/transaction features? → Use 11559 (Stallions)
└─ Infrastructure/AWS/DevOps? → Use 11561 (Infra)
Step 2: Choose Your Component (customfield_10970)
Does this work affect...
