Editing Epic Descriptions
When editing epics or stories, you need to use the --from-json flag with an "issues" array.

Example: Update Epic Description
cat > /tmp/epic-update.json << 'EOF'
{
  "issues": ["CCE-55"],
  "description": {
    "version": 1,
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {"level": 2},
        "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Summary"}]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This epic covers the implementation of..."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {"level": 2},
        "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Goals"}]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Enable feature X"}]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Improve performance"}]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
EOF

acli jira workitem edit --from-json /tmp/epic-update.json --yes
Important Notes: - The issues array can contain one or more issue keys - Use --yes to skip confirmation prompts - Cannot combine --from-json with other flags like --key or --description

